---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
---
<Base
  title="LunaTrack — period and cycle tracking"
  description="LunaTrack shows no ads on symptom logging, diary, or insights. Your device leads, the app works offline, and health data never reaches advertisers."
  path="/"
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold sm:text-4xl">LunaTrack</h1>
  <p class="mt-4 max-w-prose text-lg">
    No ads on symptom logging, your diary, or your insights — ever. That's the
    one promise here that's easy to check for yourself.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-4">
    <a href="/download" class="inline-flex h-btn-h items-center rounded-btn bg-primary px-6 text-white">
      Get it on Google Play
    </a>
    <a href="/features" class="inline-flex h-btn-h items-center rounded-btn border border-outline px-6">
      See everything it tracks
    </a>
  </div>

  <section class="mt-14 max-w-prose">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Your device leads, the cloud follows</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">
      Every log — periods, symptoms, mood, medication marks, weight, basal body
      temperature, ovulation test results — is written to a database on your
      device first. LunaTrack works fully offline, and that on-device copy is
      the source of truth; the cloud is a mirror of it, kept for the account
      LunaTrack requires so your logs follow you between devices. The device
      database is encrypted at rest with a key held in your OS keystore — the
      <a href="/privacy-policy" class="underline">full privacy policy</a> spells out
      the one documented exception to that, along with everything else that
      syncs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10 max-w-prose">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">What's inside</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">
      Predictions, a daily log for symptoms and mood, medication marks, weight
      and BBT tracking, ovulation test results, reminders, an app lock, a
      doctor-ready PDF export, and a photo timeline. The
      <a href="/features" class="underline">features page</a> covers all of it,
      including what each one does and doesn't do.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10 max-w-prose">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Ads that stay out of the way</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">
      The free version shows non-personalised ads through Google AdMob, and
      never on symptom logging, your diary, or your insights. Your health data
      is never sent to the ad network either way. If you'd rather not see ads
      at all, Premium is a one-time Google Play purchase that removes them
      everywhere — see <a href="/privacy" class="underline">how your data is handled</a>
      for the details on both.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10 max-w-prose">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Photo descriptions, off until you turn them on</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">
      The photo timeline can describe what's in a picture you choose, using an
      outside image-recognition service. That's off by default, decided
      separately for each account and each device — read
      <a href="/privacy" class="underline">what that involves</a> before you switch it on.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-14 max-w-prose border-t border-outline pt-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Try it</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">
      <a href="/download" class="underline">Download LunaTrack on Google Play</a>.
      It requires an account to sign in, and its
      <a href="/terms" class="underline">terms of use</a> cover purchases and warranty.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
